  <section id="what" aria-labelledby="what-h">
    <h2 id="what-h">What Is VO2 Max?</h2>
    <p>VO2 max is your maximum rate of oxygen use during hard exercise, measured in milliliters of oxygen per kilogram of body weight per minute. It is the single best measure of aerobic fitness and one of the strongest predictors of long-term health: in large studies, people in the lowest fitness group had several times the mortality risk of the fittest.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="chart" aria-labelledby="chart-h">
    <h2 id="chart-h">VO2 Max Chart by Age and Sex</h2>
    <figure class="tp-fig"><img src="/assets/images/vo2-max-chart-by-age.webp" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="VO2 max chart by age for men and women at the good (60th percentile) level"><figcaption>VO2 max needed for a &ldquo;good&rdquo; rating by age and sex.</figcaption></figure><div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table tp-num"><caption>Men: VO2 max norms (ml/kg/min)</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Age</th><th scope="col">Fair (40th)</th><th scope="col">Good (60th)</th><th scope="col">Excellent (80th)</th><th scope="col">Superior (95th)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">20&ndash;29</th><td>41.7</td><td>45.4</td><td>51.1</td><td>55.4</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">30&ndash;39</th><td>40.5</td><td>44.0</td><td>48.3</td><td>54.0</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">40&ndash;49</th><td>38.5</td><td>42.4</td><td>46.4</td><td>52.5</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">50&ndash;59</th><td>35.6</td><td>39.2</td><td>43.4</td><td>48.9</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">60&ndash;69</th><td>32.3</td><td>35.5</td><td>39.5</td><td>45.7</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">70&ndash;79</th><td>29.4</td><td>32.3</td><td>36.7</td><td>42.1</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table tp-num"><caption>Women: VO2 max norms (ml/kg/min)</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Age</th><th scope="col">Fair (40th)</th><th scope="col">Good (60th)</th><th scope="col">Excellent (80th)</th><th scope="col">Superior (95th)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">20&ndash;29</th><td>36.1</td><td>39.5</td><td>43.9</td><td>49.6</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">30&ndash;39</th><td>34.4</td><td>37.8</td><td>42.4</td><td>47.4</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">40&ndash;49</th><td>33.0</td><td>36.3</td><td>39.7</td><td>45.3</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">50&ndash;59</th><td>30.1</td><td>33.0</td><td>36.7</td><td>41.1</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">60&ndash;69</th><td>27.5</td><td>30.0</td><td>33.0</td><td>37.8</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">70&ndash;79</th><td>25.9</td><td>28.1</td><td>30.9</td><td>36.7</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="tp-note">Norms from The Cooper Institute. Values below the 40th percentile are rated poor.</p>
